{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/55675-separate-roman-pantheon","id":55675,"title":"Separate Roman Pantheon?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-01-22T14:03:54-08:00","id":55675,"title":"Separate Roman Pantheon?","content":"I was thinking, with the promise of several new Pantheons in Scion 2e, there's something that's been on my mind: should the Dodekatheon represent both Greek and Roman gods, or should they be separate pantheons this time around? I personally think so, because contrary to popular belief, the Romans didn't just take the Greek religion, change the names and call it their own, but instead, upon learning of the Greek gods, basically said, &quot;Hey, Mars and this Ares guy are pretty similar, maybe they're the same god&quot;. Besides, there are plenty of Roman gods with no Greek equivalent, like Janus and Pamona, and even the ones that do have some glaring differences. For example, the Romans saw Mars as a god of honourable combat and the spread of civilization, a far cry from the Greek depiction as Ares as a crazed, bloodthirsty thug (and besides, Mars was also the god of farming, and I can't exactly imagine a Scion of Ares plowing fields as his Calling).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/990-maplesamurai","name":"MapleSamurai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar990_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-22T14:33:54-08:00","id":55702,"title":"","content":"It would be worth thinking about, but it would probably turn out to be pretty tricky for the writers because the overlap with many of the gods are so great, there are relatively few myths that are exclusively Roman, and people are used to thinking of them as the same. Personally, I would be pleased if they were depicted as seperate, but with the lack of pantheons for South America, Australia, and sub-Saharan Africa, they probably won't be a top priority to start with.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not the <a href=\"https://www.theoi.com/Daimon/Alastor.html\" target=\"_blank\">daimon</a>, just a namesake.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/858-alastor","name":"Alastor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"62"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-22T14:35:51-08:00","id":55705,"title":"","content":"I think a separate pantheon is splitting hairs. Romans took a lot of deities from various other pantheons. (Mithra is the usual celebrated example) But for reference: <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interpretatio_graeca\" target=\"_blank\">Interpretatio graeca</a> <i> Some deities dating to Rome's oldest religious stratum, such as Janus and Terminus, had no Greek equivalent. Other Greek divine figures, most notably Apollo, were adopted directly into Roman culture, but underwent a distinctly Roman development, as when Augustus made Apollo one of his patron deities. In the early period, Etruscan culture played an intermediary role in transmitting Greek myth and religion to the Romans, as evidenced in the linguistic transformation of Greek Heracles to Etruscan Her[e]cle to Roman Hercules.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-22T15:07:58-08:00","id":55729,"title":"","content":"Also the Scion: Demigod book for 2e is including<i> Interpretatio romana. </i>Here's hoping to see<i> Interpretatio germanica</i><i> +</i><i> Interpretatio Christiana</i>. Although I'm<br /> hoping<i> Interpretatio Christiana </i>won't include Demonetization as the only conversion approach if it's included in demigod.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/885-bwgustaf","name":"bwgustaf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"107"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-22T15:31:24-08:00","id":55744,"title":"","content":"Demonization seems to be the usual response when dealing with earlier deities. Were I active in playing, I'd create a character that was a scion of Fenris, the wolf-god that taught organized hunting to Stone Age hunters, and became demonized later by the old Norse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-22T15:45:56-08:00","id":55754,"title":"","content":"Every storyteller has to make a personal decision about which influences to include, and which to put aside. For example, within my own game setting I prefer the idea that many of the Greek gods evolved into the Roman gods. Ares was literally a belligerent teenager who matured into the far more responsible adult, Mars.<br /> <br /> Another storyteller might decide that they are the same person, but fatebonds force them to act a different way when they are speaking to different people. A third storyteller might decide that Ares is the only real god, and the stories of Mars are nothing more than humans getting carried away. A fourth storyteller might decide that they actually are two different gods who may or may not appreciate the syncretization.<br /> <br /> Similarly, I prefer the idea that Brigid (being one of the few Tuatha to survive) became embroiled in Christian politics and embraced the role of St. Brigit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-22T15:54:22-08:00","id":55763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MapleSamurai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/55675-separate-roman-pantheon#post55675\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking, with the promise of several new Pantheons in Scion 2e, there's something that's been on my mind: should the Dodekatheon represent both Greek and Roman gods, or should they be separate pantheons this time around? I personally think so, because contrary to popular belief, the Romans didn't just take the Greek religion, change the names and call it their own, but instead, upon learning of the Greek gods, basically said, &quot;Hey, Mars and this Ares guy are pretty similar, maybe they're the same god&quot;. Besides, there are plenty of Roman gods with no Greek equivalent, like Janus and Pamona, and even the ones that do have some glaring differences. For example, the Romans saw Mars as a god of honourable combat and the spread of civilization, a far cry from the Greek depiction as Ares as a crazed, bloodthirsty thug (and besides, Mars was also the god of farming, and I can't exactly imagine a Scion of Ares plowing fields as his Calling).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Besides the fact that the truth is not as black &amp; white as you're painting it (the version of Ares you're talking about is distinctly Athenian, not shared by Peleponesian culture; Jupiter literally comes from &quot;Zeu Pater&quot; - Roman culture was built on the merging of indigenous Sabine WITH GREEK COLONISTS); you're only thinking of the religion. Scion isn't abou religion. It's about myth. And as far as the Roman myths are concerned (primarily as recorded/invented by Ovid and Virgil) the Roman Gods are the Olympians; building on or retelling Greek narratives like the Trojan War (in the case of Aeneus).<br /> <br /> That said, Roman originated Gods who weren't subsumed by Greek counterparts like Janus could very well deserve their own write up, perhaps with the idea that their previous Pantheon was conquered by the Olympians.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-22T20:27:45-08:00","id":55912,"title":"","content":"As Glamour says, the problem is that while there are some differences, primarily of personality, Greek and Roman gods tend to have the same <i>stories</i>. Ares and Mars are very, very different personalities, but they <i>do the same things</i>. They have the same stories. They have the same Legend. So how can you meaningfully split them apart? There are <i>some, </i>and actually not all that many, uniquely Roman Gods. ...who may actually be <i>Etruscan</i> instead... that you might be able to split off. And the same actually goes for the Etruscans themselves, who also got lumped in with the Greco-Romans. It's because all three societies <i>did</i> a lot of trading and mimicking, with the Romans being the &quot;latest&quot; of the three and thus tending to &quot;absorb&quot; the other two.<br /> <br /> I did actually, long ago, write up a Roman PSP based on Civitas. That was a HUGE deal for Romans, working as a group to accomplish goals. The good of the Republic/Empire being held above individuals, that kinda thing. Not that it was always practiced, but it was a big consideration in their society. I feel it works very well for them as a PSP. <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rLFF53CaSnRwC0dnLwDgfa_ojSnJkSxQVFepqsyUZSU/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Here's the Link.</a><br /> <br /> There's also an <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ZCCy7qFYRPb6sroa4UEeyWNswR0pNWzv659CJVY61QA/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Etruscan PSP</a>, in case anyone wants to check that out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T00:24:09-08:00","id":55990,"title":"","content":"I kind of like the idea of redundant Gods. Both Zeus and Jupiter running around as separate individual deities, but with mostly the same backstory.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/260-chase-variant","name":"Chase Variant","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar260_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T13:27:26-08:00","id":56325,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Alastor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/55675-separate-roman-pantheon?p=55702#post55702\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would be worth thinking about, but it would probably turn out to be pretty tricky for the writers because the overlap with many of the gods are so great, there are relatively few myths that are exclusively Roman, and people are used to thinking of them as the same. Personally, I would be pleased if they were depicted as seperate, but with the lack of pantheons for South America, Australia, and sub-Saharan Africa, they probably won't be a top priority to start with.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll admit it'll probably not be a priority considering the vast wealth of untapped pantheons elsewhere in the world, sure. Truth be told, it's not topping my pantheon wish list either (that would be the Korean and Maori pantheons), I wanted to discuss the possibility.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iry</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Every storyteller has to make a personal decision about which influences to include, and which to put aside. For example, within my own game setting I prefer the idea that many of the Greek gods evolved into the Roman gods. Ares was literally a belligerent teenager who matured into the far more responsible adult, Mars.</div> </div> </div> </div>An interesting way of explaining it. If Scion still goes with &quot;the Greek and Roman pantheons are one and the same&quot; again, I think they should at least have a sidebar acknowledging the differences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I did actually, long ago, write up a Roman PSP based on Civitas. That was a HUGE deal for Romans, working as a group to accomplish goals. The good of the Republic/Empire being held above individuals, that kinda thing. Not that it was always practiced, but it was a big consideration in their society. I feel it works very well for them as a PSP. <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rLFF53CaSnRwC0dnLwDgfa_ojSnJkSxQVFepqsyUZSU/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Here's the Link.</a></div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for sharing that, Telgar. I think I might try that out for a Roman Scion one day, if only because I think Civitas was too good for its original pantheon.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/990-maplesamurai","name":"MapleSamurai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar990_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T14:01:05-08:00","id":56351,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chase Variant</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/55675-separate-roman-pantheon?p=55990#post55990\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I kind of like the idea of redundant Gods. Both Zeus and Jupiter running around as separate individual deities, but with mostly the same backstory.</div> </div> </div> </div>Reminds me of Odin (Icelandic) and Wednesday (American) in American Gods - gods having different incarnations in different societies. I'm sure there was a Wotan in Germany; a Herian in Britain, Oski in Scandanavia; Fiolnir somewhere, a guy just going by &quot;Grimm&quot; somewhere... all embodying different personalities based on different stories and cultural perceptions of the deity.<br /> <br /> Zeus and Jupiter could easily work the same way depending on whether you're in Greece or Italy.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, there's the Rick Riordan root of having the deities divide into warring aspects within themselves that they shift between. So sometimes powerful passion-driven Zeus embodying the best and worst of Arete and Hubris flashes into stern, judgmental, disciplined Jupiter clad in robes of purple.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T14:39:27-08:00","id":56368,"title":"","content":"The whole multiple-instances-of-Gods works really well in a world where Gods are created by mortal belief. The Romans envisioned Jupiter this way, prayed to him that way, and they created Jupiter as the figure they wanted him to be. The Greeks had their own ideas and created Zeus to fit those ideas. Two Gods, one for each culture. Their Legend is the same, but their worshippers are different and since worship creates the Gods, then there are two different Gods.<br /> <br /> Iron Druid Chronicles, American Gods and the Kate Daniels series all go with this idea. It's very popular in urban fantasy stories because it makes the Gods fit more nicely into a consensual reality setting where the mass will of humanity is the strongest element of the setting, putting humans on top of the pile. Gods might be stronger than <i>individual</i> humans, but <b>Humanity</b> is the chief power of the cosmos through Specialness.<br /> <br /> Scion doesn't have that element, so it becomes considerably more difficult to have Zeus <i>and</i> Jupiter sharing the same Legends. If both of them did the <i>exact same things</i>, which one was doing it? All Myths Are True, but how does that work? Did it all happen <i>twice</i>? Or three, or four, or seven, or infinite times, once for each slightly different cult view? Massive, homogenous, organized religion is pretty dern spankin new. The Athenian Ares was already different from the Spartan Ares, does that mean there are two or more Areseses, in addition to a Mars and a Laran?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T15:05:11-08:00","id":56373,"title":"","content":"Human belief and narrative though is the force that directs Fate which does directly shape the Gods.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T15:31:12-08:00","id":56384,"title":"","content":"That chapter was really... odd and not very clear. In trying to leave Fate mysterious and ineffable, it left Fate muddy and poorly defined. Human narrative has something to do with it, via Fatebonding, but Fatebonds don't <i>create</i> Gods. They influence Gods. They don't even really do that, in RAW, since they have no teeth at all. They really affect <i>humans that are <b>around</b> Gods</i>, not the Gods themselves.<br /> <br /> Fate doesn't help explain Jupiter and Zeus having the same resume while being different Gods.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T16:56:20-08:00","id":56428,"title":"","content":"Fate is, as far as I can gather, In Game Justification For STs To Do Stuff That Doesn't Always Make Sense (IGJFSTDSTDAMS). As such, I don't think it needs to be made more explicit. It needs to be made less consistent. It should be &quot;Hey, ST, you want to do that cool thing that might not make sense? You can totally do that! Because Fate is powerful and mysterious.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Games are a way for us to explore what it means to be human, or inhuman, as the case may be. Let's all have a good time playing them.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/400-guitarsniper","name":"guitarsniper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T22:49:07-08:00","id":56591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/55675-separate-roman-pantheon?p=56384#post56384\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They don't even really do that, in RAW, since they have no teeth at all. They really affect <i>humans that are <b>around</b> Gods</i>, not the Gods themselves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just to nitpick, the RAW fate system did have some tiny teeth in the sense that any given god could accrue hundreds of dice penalty worth of reverences. Of course, since you could never have fewer dice than your permanent legend this was not a huge issue. But it could certainly be a pain in the ass if you relied on dice adders instead of success adders.<br /> <br /> Otherwise I agree. There are so many variations on gods, assuming that gods who share the same legends are actually different gods can lead to some very slippery slopes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-24T06:19:17-08:00","id":56694,"title":"","content":"There is a plan for the new edition to more on religion as well as myth. I mean, people believed - and continue to believe! - this stuff. Blood was shed for it. The least we can do is acknowledge the difference between Jupiter Optimus Maximus and the King of Olympus (even if they both wield the three thunderbolts of Tinia, because they're the same dude). When I mentioned interpretatio, I meant it in the more general sense: how other cultures (and pantheons!) look at and interpret gods has an impact, if you consider that the mythology is partially driven by the acts of Gods and Fate. We're also talking about immortal, divine beings who exist over a continuum of thousands of years. People wear a lot of hats in their mayfly lives; how many more so do the gods wear, then?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Neall Raemonn Price</span><br /> Beleaguered Scion Developer</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/137-neall","name":"Neall","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar137_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1204"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T07:39:33-08:00","id":57424,"title":"","content":"Makes me wonder. If two different Scions met a given god. Would the God look the same to both of them?<br /> <br /> <img src=\"http://www.byrnerobotics.com/forum/uploads/WilliamRoberge/2013-05-19_153721_2004-09-24_204133_Galactus.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T12:03:34-08:00","id":57557,"title":"","content":"The &quot;Trial of Reed Richards&quot; that's awesome.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T17:11:35-08:00","id":57758,"title":"","content":"Having a separate Greek and Roman pantheons is a bit messy, but then again having more than one pantheon at all gets just as messy if you take it far enough. Just how many creation myths are there for instance? While some are specifically for one people, often as not a particular myth is supposed to cover all people everywhere and that obviously leads to compatibility problems. For that matter World creation myths do the same thing and obviously they cannot all be literally true at the same time if there is any kind of exclusive linear time. Gods who are simply variants of one another are a potentially more complex problem but not impossible to resolve.<br /> <br /> If when I start the campaign I am working on I have players wanting to run Scions from both of the two or there was some other plot thread that required dealing with both pantheons in detail I would probably treat them as separate pantheons and gloss over the retread parts as suspension of disbelief (so far I do not have that problem, but the first campaign I am trying to run in the system is in very early planning stage so it might come up), otherwise I would simply leave it undefined until more was needed.<br /> <br /> The same kind of problem comes up in oWoD when running mixed &quot;splats&quot; (which is the type I ran most) if one tries to get too literal with the cosmology of the various lines, and the solution is to concentrate on the player characters in the &quot;now&quot; and let esoteric details like that slide; it should work here to an acceptable level here too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":869,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/869-bc001","user":"BC001","date":". 01-25-2014, 08:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/869-bc001","name":"BC001","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar869_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T13:16:59-08:00","id":62423,"title":"","content":"I remember tracing specific gods backwards, to see how far they went and who they were in the past. It was quite fascinating, and it makes me wonder if those predecessors still exist in Scion, and in what form. If you're going as far back as Babylon, for example, you can see some later gods who sprang up from the Babylonian mythos - while some Shinto gods were 'adopted' and evolved from Ainu gods or from Chinese gods, or from Hindu gods.<br /> <br /> I've considered, at one point, taking all the gods and tracing them back to their earliest essences. THOSE are the gods. They've adopted different names and such over time, but when you encounter them, they're more like their primeval aspects instead. The entire idea of 'pantheons' is something humanity made up, and doesn't actually exist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-01T06:30:45-08:00","id":62878,"title":"","content":"Easy enough to go backwards. Just examine the &quot;demons.&quot; Likely they were earlier gods that only became demonized to justify new deities taking over.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-01T20:11:38-08:00","id":63257,"title":"","content":"I think instead of separate write ups for over lapping Gods, just a side bar detailing how the god is perceived differently in this new light.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2164-mistershifter","name":"MisterShifter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"26"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-11T18:27:42-08:00","id":70211,"title":"","content":"I can only think of one good reason to have a seperate Greek and Roman pantheon in Scion 2.0, to make it easier to run Percy Jackson fan-games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/509-tiwaztyrsfist","name":"TiwazTyrsfist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar509_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}